What is TTL?

TTL stands for Time To Live.

It tells us:

"How many seconds are left before this key expires?"

TTL does not set the expiry.

It only reports the remaining time.

------------------------------------------------------------

Difference between EXPIRE and TTL

EXPIRE

Purpose:
Sets an expiry time for a key.

Example:

EXPIRE token 60

Meaning:

Delete this key after 60 seconds.

Internally:

Current time = 1000

↓

EXPIRE token 60

↓

expiry["token"] = 1060

EXPIRE modifies the expiry dictionary.

------------------------------------------------------------

TTL

Purpose:
Returns the remaining lifetime of a key.

Example:

Current time = 1020

expiry["token"] = 1060

TTL token

↓

1060 - 1020

↓

40

Redis returns:

(integer) 40

TTL never changes the expiry time.

It only calculates:

remaining_time = expiry_time - current_time

------------------------------------------------------------

Real-world example

User logs in.

SET session:abc xyz

EXPIRE session:abc 86400

(24 hours)

Later...

The frontend wants to know:

"How long until the user is automatically logged out?"

Instead of remembering the expiry itself, it asks Redis:

TTL session:abc

↓

3521

Meaning:

This session will expire in 3521 seconds.

------------------------------------------------------------

Another example

OTP

EXPIRE otp 300

(5 minutes)

Two minutes later:

TTL otp

↓

180

The application can now show:

"OTP expires in 3 minutes."

------------------------------------------------------------

Redis return values

TTL key

Case 1

Key exists and has expiry

↓

Returns remaining seconds.

Example:

:45

Meaning:

45 seconds left.

----------------------

Case 2

Key exists but has no expiry

↓

Returns

:-1

Meaning:

The key exists forever.

----------------------

Case 3

Key doesn't exist

↓

Returns

:-2

Meaning:

Nothing to check because the key is missing.

------------------------------------------------------------

Summary

EXPIRE

- Sets when a key should expire.
- Writes into the expiry dictionary.

TTL

- Reads from the expiry dictionary.
- Calculates remaining time.
- Does not modify anything.
- Returns the number of seconds left.